{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport urllib.request, json\ndata=json.load(urllib.request.urlopen('https://zenodo.org/api/records/17190694'))\nprint(json.dumps(data.get('metadata',{}), indent=2)[:5000])\nprint('files count',len(data.get('files',[])))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"{\n  \"title\": \"Identification of age-related CpG sites from longitudinal avian methylomes\",\n  \"doi\": \"10.5281/zenodo.17190694\",\n  \"publication_date\": \"2025-09-25\",\n  \"description\": \"<p>Sex chromosomes are thought to play an important role in sex-dependent ageing, yet they are neglected in epigenetic aging research. We identified genome-wide age-related CpG (AR-CpG) sites in two avian species (zebra finch and jackdaw) and found AR-CpG sites to be overrepresented on the haploid, female-specific W chromosome in both species, and on the Z chromosome in the zebra finch.</p>\",\n  \"access_right\": \"open\",\n  \"creators\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Tangili, Marianthi\",\n      \"affiliation\": \"University of Groningen\",\n      \"orcid\": \"0000-0002-2871-5265\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Sudyka, Joanna\",\n      \"affiliation\": \"University of Groningen\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Furni, Fabricio\",\n      \"affiliation\": \"University of Groningen\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Palsb\\u00f8ll, Per\",\n      \"affiliation\": \"University of Groningen\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Verhulst, Simon\",\n      \"affiliation\": \"University of Groningen\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"keywords\": [\n    \"Aging\",\n    \"Epigenetics\",\n    \"DNA methylation\",\n    \"Avian\",\n    \"Sex chromosomes\"\n  ],\n  \"related_identifiers\": [\n    {\n      \"identifier\": \"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/bioproject/?term=PRJNA1108628\",\n      \"relation\": \"isSupplementedBy\",\n      \"scheme\": \"url\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"identifier\": \"10.5061/dryad.wm37pvmw8\",\n      \"relation\": \"isSourceOf\",\n      \"scheme\": \"doi\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"resource_type\": {\n    \"title\": \"Software\",\n    \"type\": \"software\"\n  },\n  \"license\": {\n    \"id\": \"mit-license\"\n  },\n  \"communities\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"dryad\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"relations\": {\n    \"version\": [\n      {\n        \"index\": 2,\n        \"is_last\": true,\n        \"parent\": {\n          \"pid_type\": \"recid\",\n          \"pid_value\": \"12074858\"\n        }\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  \"notes\": \"<p>Funding provided by: University of Groningen<br>ROR ID: https://ror.org/012p63287<br>Award Number: </p><p>Funding provided by: European Commission<br>ROR ID: https://ror.org/00k4n6c32<br>Award Number: 101025890</p><p>Funding provided by: European Commission<br>ROR ID: https://ror.org/00k4n6c32<br>Award Number: 813383</p>\",\n  \"method\": \"<p>Zebra finch blood samples were collected during a long-term experiment where birds were housed in outdoor aviaries (320 \\u00d7 150 \\u00d7 210 cm) each containing single sex groups of 18\\u201324 adults\\u00a0. Twenty longitudinal samples were collected from ten known-age individuals (six males and four females) sampled twice during their lifetime at an average interval of 1,470 days between the two sampling points. Jackdaw blood samples were collected from individuals of a free-ranging population breeding in nest-boxes south of Groningen, the Netherlands (53.1708\\u00b0N, 6.6064\\u00b0E). We analyzed 22 longitudinal blood samples collected during 2007 \\u2013 2021 from 11 known age adults (five males and six females) with an average sampling interval of 2,429 days. Samples were taken between June 2008 and December 2014 and stored in -80<sup>o</sup>C in EDTA buffer.</p>\\n<p>We extracted total-cell DNA using innuPREP DNA Mini Kit (Analytik Jena GmbH+Co) from 3\\u03bcl (nucleated) red blood cells according to the manufacturer's protocol. Whole genome sequencing was performed by The Hospital for Sick Children (Toronto, Canada) where paired-end Illumina next-generation sequencing (150bp) was carried out on either an Illumina HiSeqX\\u2122 (12 zebra finch samples) or an Illumina NovaSeq\\u2122 sequencer (eight zebra finch samples and 22 jackdaw samples). Libraries were prepared using the Swift Biosciences Inc. Accel NGS Methyl Seq kit (part no. 30024 and 30096) and the DNA was bisulfite converted using the EZ-96 DNA Methylation-Gold kit from (Zymo Research Inc., part no. D5005) as per the manufacturer's protocol and subsequently subjected to whole-genome amplification. \\u00a0</p>\\n<p>Sequences were trimmed using Trim Galore! \\u00a0v. 0.6.10 (<em>38</em>) in paired-end mode. Visual quality controls of the data were carried out before and after trimming using FastQC v. 011.9 (<em>39</em>) and MultiQC v. 11.14 (<em>40</em>). Because the Swift Biosciences Inc. Accel NGS Methyl Seq kit was used for library preparation, the first ~10 bp showed extreme biases in sequence composition and M-bias, so after checking the M-bias plots, the first 10 bps were further trimmed from each sequence.</p>\\n<p>Alignments were performed using Bismark v. 0.14.433\\u00a0 using the Bowtie 2 v. 2.4.5 alignment algorithm \\u00a0for both in silico bisulfite conversion of the reference genomes and alignments. For zebra finch, trimmed reads were aligned against the in silico bisulfite converted zebra finch (<em>Taeniopygia guttata</em>) reference genome (GCA_003957565.4) and the average mapping efficiency was 64.5% (SD: 3.22). Because the jackdaw reference genome did not contain an assembled W chromosome, the jackdaw bisulfit\nfiles count 2\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
